      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the first two terms of the arithmetic sequence and then find their product. Here are the steps:\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n   - The fifth term of the arithmetic sequence is \\(11\\).\n   - The common difference \\(d\\) is \\(1\\).\n\n2. **Use the formula for the nth term of an arithmetic sequence:**\n   The nth term of an arithmetic sequence can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n   \\]\n   where \\(a_n\\) is the nth term, \\(a_1\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the term number.\n\n3. **Apply the formula to the fifth term:**\n   For the fifth term (\\(a_5\\)):\n   \\[\n   a_5 = a_1 + (5-1)d\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   11 = a_1 + 4 \\cdot 1\n   \\]\n   Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   11 = a_1 + 4\n   \\]\n   Solve for \\(a_1\\):\n   \\[\n   a_1 = 11 - 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a_1 = 7\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the first two terms:**\n   - The first term \\(a_1\\) is \\(7\\).\n   - The second term \\(a_2\\) can be found using the common difference \\(d = 1\\):\n   \\[\n   a_2 = a_1 + d\n   \\]\n   Substituting the values:\n   \\[\n   a_2 = 7 + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a_2 = 8\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the product of the first two terms:**\n   \\[\n   a_1 \\times a_2 = 7 \\times 8\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a_1 \\times a_2 = 56\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the first two terms is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
