      "text": "Let the first term be \\(a\\) and the common difference be \\(d\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by the formula:\n\\[\na_n = a + (n-1)d\n\\]\nWe know that the fifth term (\\(a_5\\)) is 11, so we can write:\n\\[\na + 4d = 11\n\\]\nWe are also given that the difference between two consecutive terms is 1, which means \\(d = 1\\). Substituting \\(d = 1\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\na + 4 \\cdot 1 = 11\n\\]\n\\[\na + 4 = 11\n\\]\n\\[\na = 7\n\\]\nThe first two terms of the sequence are \\(a = 7\\) and \\(a + d = 7 + 1 = 8\\). Therefore, the product of the first two terms is:\n\\[\n7 \\times 8 = 56\n\\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{56}\n\\]",
